---
import { Picture } from "astro:assets";

import articleHero from "../../../../assets/beer-application-report-hero.png";
import LandingFooter from "../../../../components/LandingFooter.astro";
import LandingHeader from "../../../../components/LandingHeader.astro";
import { BEER_APPLICATION_REPORT_ARTICLE_EN } from "../../../../content/articles";
import { buildArticlePageGraph } from "../../../../lib/seo";
import { readPublicSiteConfig } from "../../../../lib/site-config";
import BaseLayout from "../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const article = BEER_APPLICATION_REPORT_ARTICLE_EN;
const config = readPublicSiteConfig({ PUBLIC_PHONE: import.meta.env.PUBLIC_PHONE });
---

<BaseLayout metadata={article} graph={buildArticlePageGraph(article)}>
  <LandingHeader page={article} phone={config.phone} />
  <main id="main" class="article-page">
    <header class="article-hero">
      <div class="container article-hero__inner">
        <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/en/">Markiro</a></li><li aria-current="page">{article.navigationLabel}</li></ol></nav>
        <div class="article-hero__layout">
          <div class="article-hero__copy">
            <p class="section-index">{article.eyebrow}</p>
            <h1>{article.heading}</h1>
            <p class="article-hero__lead">{article.introduction}</p>
            <div class="article-byline" aria-label="Publication details"><span>{article.authorName}</span><time datetime={article.publishedAt}>August 27, 2026</time><span>{article.readingTimeMinutes} min read</span></div>
          </div>
          <figure class="article-hero__media" data-article-visual>
            <Picture src={articleHero} alt="Production manager and operator reconciling a shift before a beer code application report" widths={[480, 720, 1080]} formats={["avif", "webp"]} sizes="(max-width: 767px) 100vw, 45vw" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" data-article-hero-image />
            <figcaption>The document starts with a reconciled production fact: which physical units were actually completed and accepted.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <article class="container article-body">
      <aside class="article-summary" aria-labelledby="article-summary-title">
        <p class="section-index">In brief</p><h2 id="article-summary-title">What matters in a beer code application report</h2>
        <ul>
          <li data-key-takeaway>The report confirms that marking codes were physically applied and used on produced goods.</li>
          <li data-key-takeaway>For beer produced in Russia, the domestic commissioning document is generated from the application report.</li>
          <li data-key-takeaway>The source list contains only eligible codes from a closed shift, excluding duplicates, rejects, and undone events.</li>
          <li data-key-takeaway>An automatically created draft is not a submitted report: it still requires review, submission, and signature.</li>
          <li data-key-takeaway>A partial result is retried only for corrected failed codes; a full error requires the complete corrected set.</li>
          <li data-key-takeaway>Markiro prepares auditable shift data, while direct SUZ submission must be integrated and accepted separately.</li>
        </ul>
      </aside>

      <nav class="article-toc" aria-label="Table of contents"><p class="section-index">Contents</p><ol><li><a href="#purpose">Purpose</a></li><li><a href="#deadline">Submission deadline</a></li><li><a href="#source">Eligible codes</a></li><li><a href="#fields">Fields and code format</a></li><li><a href="#submit">Submission and signature</a></li><li><a href="#statuses">Processing statuses</a></li><li><a href="#reconcile">Final reconciliation</a></li><li><a href="#markiro">Markiro boundary</a></li></ol></nav>

      <section id="purpose" aria-labelledby="purpose-title">
        <p class="section-index">01 / Purpose</p><h2 id="purpose-title">What does the Russian beer code application report confirm?</h2>
        <p><strong>The report tells the Russian marking system which ordered codes were physically applied and used on products.</strong> For beer and low-alcohol beverages produced in Russia, Chestny ZNAK states that the domestic commissioning document is generated automatically when the application report is formed.</p>
        <p>The source therefore cannot be a list of everything sent to the printer. A label may have jammed, an item may have been rejected, a scan may have been undone, or a case disassembled. The report needs the final production state after those exceptions.</p>
        <p>The authoritative procedure supports manual work in SUZ and integration through SUZ API 3.0. See Chestny ZNAK's current guide on <a href="https://markirovka.ru/knowledge/tovarnye-gruppy/pivo-pivniye-napitki/kak-otpravit-otchet-o-nanesenii-pivo">submitting a beer application report</a>. Interface labels and code statuses remain in Russian, so an English implementation guide should retain a bilingual operational glossary.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="deadline" aria-labelledby="deadline-title">
        <p class="section-index">02 / Deadline</p><h2 id="deadline-title">When must a Russian producer submit the report?</h2>
        <p><strong>The current official beer guidance says a producer submits after applying the identification means and no later than one calendar day from the production date.</strong> Treat this as a mandatory shift-closing control, not a weekly clerical batch.</p>
        <p>The same source describes separate importer deadlines and code lifetimes based on the selected payment method. Confirm the current Russian rule before each implementation or compliance change: <a href="https://markirovka.ru/knowledge/tovarnye-gruppy/pivo-pivniye-napitki/kogda-neobkhodimo-podavat-otchet-o-nanesenii-pivo">official beer application-report timing</a>.</p>
        <div class="article-callout"><strong>The operational checkpoint is a closed and reconciled shift.</strong><p>A draft created before exception handling finishes may include a duplicate, a damaged label, or an item the operator later removed.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section id="source" aria-labelledby="source-title">
        <p class="section-index">03 / Source</p><h2 id="source-title">Which marking codes belong in the report?</h2>
        <p><strong>Use the authoritative accepted-code registry for the closed shift, not the number of print jobs.</strong> Each code must retain product and GTIN, production date, shift, local verdict, and current operation state. A repeated scan does not create another item, and an undo must remove that code from the reportable set.</p>
        <figure class="article-visual" data-article-visual>
          <img src="/images/articles/beer-application-report-source-en.svg" width="1200" height="620" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Five stages for preparing codes from a closed production shift for the Russian beer application report" data-article-diagram />
          <figcaption>Close production, select authoritative codes, remove exceptions, reconcile totals, and only then build the draft.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Before building the draft, verify four controls: eligible code count equals accepted physical units; each identity appears once; items in closed cases match the saved case membership; and no SSCC or case composition changed after the snapshot. Our guide to a <a href="/en/articles/duplicate-beer-marking-code-russia/">duplicate beer marking code</a> covers conflict classification.</p>
        <p>Record the source snapshot time. If a manager later disassembles a case, undoes a scan, or resolves a cross-terminal conflict, the old file no longer represents the final state. Generate and reconcile a new snapshot instead of silently deleting one line from the previous export.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="fields" aria-labelledby="fields-title">
        <p class="section-index">04 / Data</p><h2 id="fields-title">Which fields and code sequences need validation?</h2>
        <p><strong>Confirm that the product record is ready for commissioning and that common fields match the physical production run.</strong> Current beer workflows include organization and activity-location data, actual alcohol content, the primary production accounting document and date, and actual volume where required for kegs.</p>
        <p>For manual upload, Chestny ZNAK describes a full marking-code sequence containing AI 01, GTIN, AI 21, serial, the GS control separator, AI 93, and verification code. GS is ASCII 29, not two printable characters. Losing it in a spreadsheet or text transformation changes the payload. The official guide explains <a href="https://markirovka.ru/knowledge/tovarnye-gruppy/pivo-pivniye-napitki/chto-delat-esli-ne-sozdalsya-chernovik-otcheta-o-nanesenii-pivo">manual report creation when no draft appears</a>.</p>
        <div class="article-callout article-callout--boundary"><strong>Do not reconstruct the full code from a picture or truncated export.</strong><p>A source without the verification segment and GS separator is not suitable for a full application report. Use the protected source or a verified scan.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section id="submit" aria-labelledby="submit-title">
        <p class="section-index">05 / Submission</p><h2 id="submit-title">Draft, submission, and signature are different states</h2>
        <p><strong>An automatically generated draft still has to be reviewed, saved, submitted, and signed with the appropriate certificate.</strong> An unsigned document remains pending. Obtaining the same previously ordered codes again does not require another application report.</p>
        <ol class="article-steps">
          <li><h3>Review the draft</h3><p>Compare product, production date, accounting document, and code count with the closed shift.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Validate the code set</h3><p>Exclude duplicates, rejects, and undo events; preserve every control separator.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Submit and sign</h3><p>Record document ID, time, certificate, and the system's response.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Wait for processing</h3><p>Do not treat a missing response as rejection or create a blind duplicate document.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section id="statuses" aria-labelledby="statuses-title">
        <p class="section-index">06 / Result</p><h2 id="statuses-title">How processing status changes the recovery action</h2>
        <p><strong>The next step depends on the document outcome, not on the generic word “error.”</strong> A successful report moves Russian-produced codes to the Russian status “В обороте”. With partial processing, only error-free codes change status; with a full processing error, none of the document's code statuses change.</p>
        <figure class="article-visual" data-article-visual>
          <img src="/images/articles/beer-application-report-statuses-en.svg" width="1200" height="620" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Actions after pending, successful, partial, and failed processing of a Russian beer application report" data-article-diagram />
          <figcaption>A partial result is retried for failed codes only; a full error requires the complete corrected set.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Open the document card and inspect the event result for every code. For “Обработан частично”, preserve accepted codes and submit only corrected failures. For “Обработан с ошибкой”, correct the root cause and create a new report containing the complete corrected code set.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="reconcile" aria-labelledby="reconcile-title">
        <p class="section-index">07 / Reconciliation</p><h2 id="reconcile-title">How to close the control after processing</h2>
        <p><strong>Retain the chain shift → source snapshot → document → per-code outcome.</strong> A green document label is not enough: the final accepted-code count must match physical output after rejects and exceptions.</p>
        <div class="article-checklist">
          <div><h3>Production</h3><ul><li>closed shift;</li><li>production date;</li><li>physical units;</li><li>excluded rejects.</li></ul></div>
          <div><h3>Document</h3><ul><li>ID and time;</li><li>signature;</li><li>code count;</li><li>error file.</li></ul></div>
          <div><h3>Outcome</h3><ul><li>accepted codes;</li><li>failed codes;</li><li>follow-up report;</li><li>remaining variance.</li></ul></div>
        </div>
        <p>If a report has succeeded but an allowed attribute must change, use the designated correction document instead of another application report. The scope and restrictions are described in the official <a href="https://markirovka.ru/knowledge/tovarnye-gruppy/pivo-pivniye-napitki/instruktsiya-po-korrektirovke-svedeniy-o-kodakh-v-sisteme-markirovki-pivo">beer code-data correction guidance</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="markiro" aria-labelledby="markiro-title">
        <p class="section-index">08 / Markiro</p><h2 id="markiro-title">What Markiro prepares for external reporting</h2>
        <p><strong>Markiro builds an auditable source from a closed shift: authoritative accepted codes, production date, product, and—when aggregation is used—closed case contents with SSCCs.</strong> TXT and CSV shift exports are available in flat and case-aware forms; a separate XML format represents an aggregation report.</p>
        <p>The current aggregation XML is not an application report. Direct submission through SUZ API, electronic signature handling, and status ingestion require a separate integration, certificate setup, and acceptance scenario. Until then, Markiro is the production source and the application report is transferred through the agreed external workflow.</p>
        <p>This boundary allows a smaller producer to start without an oversized integration project: operators receive a clean shift source and control totals, while API submission can be added after the process is stable. See our guide to a staged <a href="/en/articles/beer-marking-cost-russia/">beer marking budget</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-conclusion" aria-labelledby="conclusion-title">
        <p class="section-index">Conclusion</p><h2 id="conclusion-title">A reliable application report begins with a reconciled shift</h2>
        <p>Establish the physically produced units, remove duplicates and undone events, reconcile cases, and only then review and sign the document. After processing, reconcile each code outcome so the report remains a provable continuation of production.</p>
        <a class="button" href="/en/#demo" data-analytics="landing_demo_click" data-placement="article-conclusion">Review your shift-closing flow <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>

      <section class="article-related" aria-labelledby="article-related-title">
        <p class="section-index">Related</p><h2 id="article-related-title">Continue configuring the production workflow</h2>
        <div class="seo-related__grid"><a href="/en/articles/duplicate-beer-marking-code-russia/"><span>Duplicate code diagnosis</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a href="/en/articles/offline-beer-marking-russia/"><span>Offline beer marking</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a href="/en/articles/beer-case-aggregation/"><span>Beer case aggregation</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
  <LandingFooter page={article} />
  <script>import { browserLandingRuntime, initLanding } from "../../../../scripts/site"; initLanding(document, browserLandingRuntime(window));</script>
</BaseLayout>
